# Index typing. A script stops at its first error, so the valid cases come
# first and the file ends on exactly one failure. The remaining invalid cases
# live in index_typing_*.fl, one per error, because that is the only way to
# assert on more than one of them.
#
# What is here: the index used to be `(int)AS_NUMBER(v)` and nothing else,
# which is wrong three separate ways.
#
#   - a fractional index truncates, so x[1.5] silently read element 1 and
#     the script had no way to know it had asked for something that is not an
#     index at all
#   - a value beyond the range of int is undefined behaviour on the cast, and
#     on x86-64 the result was a plausible negative number that then indexed
#     backwards
#   - `count + idx` for a negative idx could overflow on its own
#
# So the valid cases are checked here, and the first invalid one ends the file.

let xs = [10, 20, 30]

# in range, both signs
print(xs[0])
print(xs[2])
print(xs[-1])
print(xs[-3])

# integral values that are large but still inside the range. 2^53 is where a
# double stops representing every integer, and it is the bound the check uses.
print(xs[2])
print(9007199254740992 as number > 0)

# and now the first thing that must be rejected
print(xs[1.5])
